Apollo @PROJECT_VERSION@
A cross-platform wavetable synthesizer by ProdByRnV

WHAT IS IN THIS PACKAGE

  VST3/Apollo.vst3        the plugin, for a DAW
  Standalone/             Apollo as an application, needing no DAW
  INSTALL.txt             this file

  Nothing here is an installer. Apollo is a folder you copy, and removing it
  is deleting that folder again.


INSTALLING THE PLUGIN

  Copy the whole "Apollo.vst3" folder — not the file inside it — into the
  place your system keeps VST3 plugins:

  Windows     C:\Program Files\Common Files\VST3
              or, without administrator rights:
              %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Common\VST3

  macOS       /Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3
              or, for one user only:
              ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3

  Linux       /usr/lib/vst3
              or, for one user only:
              ~/.vst3

  Then rescan your plugins in your DAW. Most hosts do this on startup; some
  have a button for it.


RUNNING THE STANDALONE

  Open the application in the Standalone folder. It has its own audio and
  MIDI settings, and does not need a DAW or the plugin to be installed.


WHAT APOLLO NEEDS

  A 64-bit system. On Windows, Windows 10 or later. On macOS, 11 or later.

  Apollo does not require an internet connection, and it does not phone
  anywhere while you use it.

  Windows

    The Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime, which draws Apollo's interface.
    Windows 11 has it already; Windows 10 usually does, because it arrives
    with Microsoft Edge. If Apollo's window opens without an interface,
    install "Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime" from Microsoft and reopen it.

    You do NOT need the Visual C++ Redistributable. Apollo carries what it
    needs of that runtime inside itself.

  macOS

    Nothing beyond the system. Everything Apollo uses is part of macOS.

  Linux

    Apollo loads these while it runs, so a minimal or headless system needs
    them installed. Without the WebKitGTK set, Apollo still loads and still
    makes sound: it is the interface that needs it.

      X11         libX11.so.6, libXext.so.6, libXcursor.so.1,
                  libXinerama.so.1, libXrender.so.1, libXrandr.so.2
      Fonts       libfreetype.so.6, libfontconfig.so.1
      Audio       libasound.so.2      (the standalone; the plugin uses the host's)
      Interface   libgtk-3.so.0, libglib-2.0.so.0, and one WebKitGTK
                  generation — either libwebkit2gtk-4.1.so.0 with
                  libjavascriptcoregtk-4.1.so.0 and libsoup-3.0.so.0,
                  or libwebkit2gtk-4.0.so.37 with
                  libjavascriptcoregtk-4.0.so.18 and libsoup-2.4.so.1

    On Debian and Ubuntu:

      sudo apt install libx11-6 libxext6 libxcursor1 libxinerama1 \
                       libxrender1 libxrandr2 libfreetype6 libfontconfig1 \
                       libasound2 libgtk-3-0 libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0

    On Fedora:

      sudo dnf install libX11 libXext libXcursor libXinerama libXrender \
                       libXrandr freetype fontconfig alsa-lib gtk3 \
                       webkit2gtk4.1

    On Arch:

      sudo pacman -S libx11 libxext libxcursor libxinerama libxrender \
                     libxrandr freetype2 fontconfig alsa-lib gtk3 webkit2gtk-4.1


WHERE APOLLO KEEPS YOUR THINGS

  Presets you save go to your own settings folder, not into this folder, so
  replacing Apollo with a newer version never touches them:

  Windows     %APPDATA%\Apollo\Presets
  macOS       ~/Library/Apollo/Presets
  Linux       ~/.config/Apollo/Presets

  The sounds Apollo ships with are built into the plugin itself. They cannot
  be lost, and they are there the first time you open it.


VERSION

  Apollo @PROJECT_VERSION@, built for @APOLLO_PACKAGE_SYSTEM@ on @APOLLO_PACKAGE_ARCH@.
